# What is a life worth in money, and who decided that?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical","b":"gemini:Charon | a clear, warm university lecturer: measured, engaged, never theatrical"}
next: {"title":"Why model at all - and which model shape fits which problem?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/health-technology-assessment-deciding/why-model-at-all-and-which-model-shape.cast"}

## When You Just Ask People
voice: female
level: basic
domain: {"x":[0,100],"y":[0,160]}
sources:
    - id: desvousges1993
      title: 'Measuring natural resource damages with contingent valuation: tests of validity and reliability. In: Hausman JA (ed.), Contingent Valuation: A Critical Assessment'
      authors: Desvousges WH, Johnson FR, Dunford RW, Boyle KJ, Hudson SP, Wilson KN
      year: 1993
      finding: Stated willingness to pay to save 2,000, 20,000 and 200,000 migratory birds was about $80, $78 and $88 — insensitive to scope.
    - id: listgallet2001
      title: What experimental protocol influence disparities between actual and hypothetical stated values? Environmental and Resource Economics 20:241–254
      authors: List JA, Gallet CA
      year: 2001
      finding: Across 29 experimental studies, hypothetical values exceeded actual payments; median ratio about 1.35, mean about 3.
    - id: diamondhausman1994
      title: 'Contingent valuation: is some number better than no number? Journal of Economic Perspectives 8(4):45–64'
      authors: Diamond PA, Hausman JA
      year: 1994
      finding: Argues contingent valuation measures moral satisfaction rather than economic value.
    - id: hanemann1994
      title: Valuing the environment through contingent valuation. Journal of Economic Perspectives 8(4):19–43
      authors: Hanemann WM
      year: 1994
      finding: Defends contingent valuation; refusing to estimate implicitly values non-market goods at zero.
    - id: noaa1993
      title: Report of the NOAA Panel on Contingent Valuation. Federal Register 58(10):4601–4614
      authors: Arrow K, Solow R, Portney PR, Leamer EE, Radner R, Schuman H
      year: 1993
      finding: Contingent valuation can be reliable enough for damage assessment if strict guidelines are followed, including a scope test.

    axes ax x_label "Scale of the good (birds saved)" y_label "Stated WTP ($ per household)" hidden true
    curve expected dashed #8f887c expr "80 + 1.05*(x - 15)" x_from 15 x_to 85 hidden true
    label label_expected "If scope mattered" above-left #8f887c attach_to expected hidden true
    dot p1 #b5482e at.x 15 at.y 80 hidden true
    label l1 "2,000 birds: $80" below #b5482e attach_to p1 hidden true
    dot p2 #b5482e at.x 50 at.y 78 hidden true
    label l2 "20,000 birds: $78" below #b5482e attach_to p2 hidden true
    dot p3 #b5482e at.x 85 at.y 88 hidden true
    label l3 "200,000 birds: $88" below #b5482e attach_to p3 cites ["desvousges1993"] hidden true
    curve actual #b5482e expr "82" x_from 15 x_to 85 hidden true
    label label_actual "What people actually said" above #b5482e attach_to actual hidden true
    box n_q "What would you pay?" x 500 y 620 width 360 height 74 font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_warm "Warm glow" #b5482e x 180 y 420 width 270 height 74 font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_protest "Protest zero" #b5482e x 500 y 420 width 270 height 74 font_size 30 hidden true
    box n_hypo "Hypothetical yes" #b5482e x 820 y 420 width 300 height 74 font_size 30 hidden true
    edge e_warm n_q -> n_warm hidden true
    edge e_protest n_q -> n_protest hidden true
    edge e_hypo n_q -> n_hypo hidden true
    text l_hypo "Median ≈ 1.35× real" #b5482e cites ["listgallet2001"] x 820 y 345 font_size 26 hidden true
    box n_dh "Diamond & Hausman (1994)" #b5482e cites ["diamondhausman1994"] x 250 y 430 width 420 height 74 font_size 30 hidden true
    text l_dh "Moral satisfaction, not value" #b5482e x 250 y 355 font_size 26 hidden true
    box n_han "Hanemann (1994)" #2f6b8f cites ["hanemann1994"] x 760 y 430 width 320 height 74 font_size 30 hidden true
    text l_han "Zero is also a number" #2f6b8f x 760 y 355 font_size 26 hidden true
    box n_noaa "NOAA panel (1993)" #8a5fa8 cites ["noaa1993"] x 500 y 600 width 340 height 74 font_size 30 hidden true
    text l_noaa "Usable, under strict rules" #8a5fa8 x 500 y 680 font_size 26 hidden true
    edge e_noaa_dh n_noaa -> n_dh hidden true
    edge e_noaa_han n_noaa -> n_han hidden true

A payslip is one way to read a price of life; the obvious alternative is simply to ask people what a benefit is worth.
    draw ax

If people were pricing the good on offer, willingness to pay should climb steeply as the good gets bigger — like this dashed line.
    draw expected label_expected

A real experiment: American households were asked what they would pay to stop migratory birds drowning in uncovered oil ponds — two thousand birds, about eighty dollars a household.
    draw p1 l1

    pause 0.3

Ten times the birds: seventy-eight dollars. A hundred times the birds: eighty-eight.
    draw p2 l2 p3 l3

Desvousges and colleagues found the answers flat — scope insensitivity, the single most damaging finding about contingent valuation.
    draw actual label_actual delivery grave

    quiz "Raising the birds at risk a hundredfold changed stated willingness to pay by how much?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Essentially not at all — eighty dollars for two thousand birds, eighty-eight for two hundred thousand.
        wrong: The whole force of the study is that the number barely moved.
        * Roughly a hundredfold
        * Roughly double
        + Essentially not at all

What is being priced is the warm glow of helping birds, and one purchase of moral satisfaction costs about what another does.
    focus p1 p2 p3 actual

    pause 0.4

    clear

The failures are patterned, and they follow from what the question asks of a respondent: name a price, on the spot, for something you will never be billed for.
    draw n_q

Route one, the warm glow: you answer how much you care, not what the thing delivers.
    draw n_warm e_warm

Route two, the protest zero: some refuse to name a price at all, meaning wrong question, not worthless — drop those answers and the mean rises, keep them and it falls, and no rule tells you which.
    draw n_protest e_protest

Route three, hypothetical bias: nothing leaves the wallet, so the answer is free to be generous.
    draw n_hypo e_hypo

List and Gallet's meta-analysis of twenty-nine experiments put the median hypothetical value at about one point three five times the real payment, with the mean near three — a long tail of wild overstatement.
    draw l_hypo

    quiz "Compared with real payments, hypothetical answers are usually:"
        right: Higher — the median study overstates by about a third, the average by roughly threefold.
        wrong: They run high: talk is cheaper than money.
        * Lower
        * About the same
        + Higher

    pause 0.4

    clear

Diamond and Hausman, in 1994, argued that these surveys measure moral satisfaction, and that no number is better than a bad number.
    draw n_dh l_dh

Hanemann replied in the same journal issue that ordinary markets misbehave too, and that refusing to estimate simply sets the value of a species — or a life — to zero by default.
    draw n_han l_han

After the Exxon Valdez spill, a panel chaired by Kenneth Arrow and Robert Solow had already ruled in 1993 that the method could be reliable enough for court — but only under strict rules, including a test that answers respond to scope.
    draw n_noaa e_noaa_dh e_noaa_han l_noaa

    pause 0.4

So asking Norwegians what they would pay for nusinersen would measure their compassion honestly, and the drug's value not at all: a hypothetical question buys a hypothetical price.
    text verdict "A hypothetical question buys a hypothetical price" x 500 y 250 font_size 30
    mark mark_verdict target verdict kind box

## What the QALY Cannot Hold
voice: female
level: basic
use: venn_diagram
with:
    sets:
      - {label: 'Cost-utility: QALYs', only_label: Equal QALYs}
      - {label: 'Cost-benefit: kroner', only_label: Dignity · process}
    overlap_labels:
      ab: Health gains
    shade:
      - ab
      - b
    universe_box: true
    title: What each method can hold
sources:
    - id: dotvsl2021
      title: Departmental Guidance on Valuation of a Statistical Life in Economic Analysis — 2021 update
      authors: US Department of Transportation
      year: 2021
      finding: VSL of $11.6 million (2020 dollars) for analyses using a 2020 base year.
      url: https://www.transportation.gov/resources/value-of-a-statistical-life-guidance

The road and the clinic price the same life differently — so which method can even hold what we care about?
    draw universe title

Cost-utility counts in one currency only: QALYs.
    draw set_0 set_label_0

    pause 0.3

Cost-benefit draws a wider circle — anything people will pay kroner for, health or not.
    draw set_1 set_label_1

    pause 0.3

They overlap on health gains, and for most appraisals that overlap is the whole analysis.
    draw shade_ab region_label_ab

Nusinersen for spinal muscular atrophy lives here: real motor function and survival, against a list price near 750,000 dollars in year one and 375,000 a year after.
    highlight shade_ab effect glow

Beslutningsforum's no in 2017, and the later yes for patients under eighteen, was argued almost entirely inside this overlap.
    focus shade_ab region_label_ab

    pause 0.5

But out here sits everything cost-benefit will still price and the QALY simply cannot: dignity, information, the process of care itself.
    draw shade_b region_label_b

A test that changes no treatment yields zero QALYs — yet parents who finally have a name for their child's condition will pay for that answer, and a willingness-to-pay study records it.
    focus region_label_b shade_b

Process utility is the same trick: being told, being asked, waiting less — value in how care is delivered, not only in what it delivers.
    point at.ref region_label_b gesture underline

That breadth is why a transport regulator is comfortable with enormous money values — the US Department of Transportation used about 11.6 million dollars per statistical life in 2021 — while health agencies stay in a health-only currency.
    highlight set_1 effect glow

    quiz "Which of these does a cost-utility analysis, by construction, leave out?" intro "A quick check."
        right: Information with no treatment consequence produces no QALYs, so cost-utility records nothing — while a willingness-to-pay study can put kroner on it.
        wrong: The other two are health changes, and QALYs were built precisely to capture those.
        * A gain in life expectancy
        + A diagnosis that changes no treatment
        * A drop in quality of life

And the breadth has a price of its own: a QALY counts the same whoever gains it, while willingness to pay rises with income.
    draw region_label_a

Welfarists say only money can compare health against roads and schools; extra-welfarists reply that willingness to pay smuggles the income distribution into the decision — unresolved, so Norway runs both systems side by side.
    highlight set_0 set_1 effect pulse

    pause 0.5

    clear

So a rule of thumb for any monetary life-value you meet. First: whose preferences, and from which choice — a wage premium, a survey, or a budget?
    text card_title "Reading any life-value in money" x 500 y 560 font_size 34
    text q1 "1. Whose preferences, from what choice?" x 500 y 450 font_size 28

Second: is it pricing health, or ability to pay?
    text q2 "2. Health — or ability to pay?" x 500 y 370 font_size 28

Third: what gets displaced to honour it — the patients you never meet.
    text q3 "3. What is displaced to honour it?" x 500 y 290 font_size 28

Across this series: money holds what the QALY drops, but only in a currency nobody agrees on. A price on a life was never a fact about the life — it records who was asked, and what they had to give up.
    mark mark_q3 target q3 kind box
    delivery grave

